/* book exam traps: Mixing up the ports: the pitot port feeds only the airspeed capsule, while the static port feeds the airspeed capsule, altimeter and vertical speed indicator. | Reading indicated airspeed as true airspeed. At altitude, the same IAS corresponds to a higher TAS because the air density is lower. | Forgetting that the altimeter reads height above the pressure level set in its subscale, not height above the ground or above sea level unless QNH is set correctly. | Thinking a blocked pitot always makes the ASI read zero. With static pressure still changing, a blocked pitot can make the ASI climb when the aircraft climbs. | Using 1013.25 hPa as QNH for landing. It gives pressure altitude; QNH must come from the aerodrome pressure report. */
